# Line endings for platform-specific scripts.
#
# Only the file types below are pinned. Everything else keeps Git's default
# handling; this is deliberately not a repository-wide normalization.
#
# cmd.exe walks a batch file by byte offset rather than by line. With LF-only
# endings a `goto` can resume mid-line, so a label lookup silently lands in the
# wrong place instead of failing cleanly. start.bat and stop.bat both use
# labels, so they have to reach Windows with CRLF whatever the contributor's
# platform or core.autocrlf setting. uninstall/uninstall.ps1 is read by
# PowerShell on the same machines and is held to the same rule.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# POSIX shells treat a trailing CR as part of the shebang or the command, so a
# CRLF checkout breaks these outright ("bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M"). Pin
# them to LF so a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true cannot convert them.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same hazard, no extension to match on. These five carry shebangs and are run
# directly, but `*.sh` cannot see them, so a default Git-for-Windows clone
# (core.autocrlf=true) converts them and they die with `bad interpreter: ...^M`.
# A new extensionless script under scripts/ needs a line here; the launcher
# test fails until it has one.
scripts/check-capability-sync text eol=lf
scripts/find-atomics text eol=lf
scripts/ora-cost-summary text eol=lf
scripts/ora-test text eol=lf
scripts/vet-prompt text eol=lf
